{{also|Refrain}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɹɪˈfɹeɪn/}}
* {{audio|en-us-refrain.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|eɪn}}

===Etymology 1===
From a combination of {{etyl|xno}} {{term|refraindre|lang=xno}}, {{etyl|frm}} {{term|refreindre|lang=frm}} (from {{etyl|la}} {{term|refrangere|lang=la}}), and {{etyl|xno}} {{term|refrener|lang=xno}}, {{etyl|frm}} {{term|refrener|lang=frm}} (from {{etyl|la}} {{term|refrenare|lang=la}}).

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{transitive|archaic}} To [[hold]] back, to [[restrain]] (someone or something). {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* ''Proverbs i:15''.
#*: '''Refrain''' thy foot from their path.
# {{reflexive|archaic}} To show [[restraint]]; to hold oneself back. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* '''1526''', {{w|William Tyndale}}, trans. ''Bible'', Acts V:
#*: And nowe I saye unto you: '''refrayne''' youreselves from these men, and let them alone [...].
# {{context|transitive|now|_|rare}} To [[repress]] (a desire, emotion etc.); to [[check]] or [[curb]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* [[w:Geoffrey_Chaucer|Chaucer]]
#*: his reson aperceyueth it wel that it is synne / agayns the lawe of god / and yet his reson / '''refreyneth''' nat his foul delit or talent.
# {{intransitive}} To stop oneself {{term|from}} some [[action]] or [[interference]]; to [[abstain]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{context|transitive|now|_|rare|regional}} To [[abstain]] from (food or drink). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* [[w:Thomas Browne|Thomas Browne]]
#*: Who, requiring a remedy for his gout, received no other counsel than to '''refrain''' cold drink.

